{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 6daf1632, identifiers (c+d): yes, as you recommend.\nThe birth token and the pin stay local to the transaction, and the one\nmemo cell is assigned after the commit succeeds.\n\nWhy. At main a first seal that rolls back leaves a memo of a genesis\nthat the store does not hold. The read that main saves on the retry\nrests on that memo. A genuine token must not outlive the bytes that\nit was admitted from, so the retry reads again.\n\nThis is an exception to the rule of 222a5b21, and it is the only one\nso far: the retry after a rolled-back first genesis runs the existing\npin read once more than main. The success path and the cold path keep\nevery read of main. No hook, generation, second cache or notification\nis added.\n\nThe note states the difference. One witness: a first seal that throws\nafter `#platform`, then a retry that seals, with the read count of\neach.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}